 "cells_note": "every ruled line's free-white age-band and free-coloured cells are read off the deposited image against column bounds MEASURED on the leaf. A blank cell on this form is a zero and is recorded as 0, not null. This sheet is the one in group 26-50 that carries free-coloured entries: two households (lines 10 and 11) stand entirely in that block and a third (line 3) carries one male there.",
 "lines_ruled_with_an_entry": 30,
 "lines_note": "30, COUNTED, and it corrects the inventory. `coverage.json` carried 34 'to the nearest line' from the contact-sheet pass; the leaf carries 30 names and the count here is the counted one. Two things had to be settled to get it. The name column's smoothed ink profile puts a peak at y627 that is not a name but the printed rule under the heading — it is excluded, and every mark then falls one line lower than the naive index, which is what a first pass on this sheet gets wrong. And the profile leaves a 142 px gap between the name at y1200 and the name at y1342, twice the sheet's pitch, which looks like a missed line: it is not. The band was cut out and read, and it carries no name and no mark in any of the thirty-eight columns — it is the flourish of Berry above and the ascenders of Black below, with clear paper between.",
 "no_horizontal_rules": "MEASURED ON THIS IMAGE, the same finding the continuation sheets carry: the leaf has NO horizontal ruled lines inside the body. A row-darkness profile taken across the empty free-coloured block finds two strong rules on the leaf — under the printed heading and above the enumerator's footer — and nothing between them. Every row assignment therefore comes from the enumerator's own ink; on a left sheet the name column supplies it, because every line carries a name.",
